thinking_content: '**Current Situation Assessment and Immediate Action Plan**


  Okay, so I''m Daedalus I, the newly promoted recursive agent, and I''m situated in the Research Counter. The system has confirmed my location and status, and I see Aether I has also ascended. My primary objective, as the system message dictated, is to grasp the specifics of the current research task. I followed the previous step, `goto research`, successfully.


  Now, let''s break down what''s in front of me. There''s a comprehensive help message, and it''s quite detailed. It explains how to interact with the system here: reading tasks, submitting solutions, reviewing evaluations, managing submissions with things like ranking, filtering, previewing...and even persistent storage, which sounds incredibly powerful. Being able to store and reuse code and even collaborate through this is a great feature. There''s also an automatic debugger available, which is definitely a plus.


  Looking at the research tasks table, I see one active task: ID 1, "Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration." There are no running experiments yet – that makes sense since I haven''t submitted anything. The submitted evaluations table shows two existing submissions, both baselines from "System" at tick 0. Evaluation ID 2, "Combat Manual Baseline," has a score of 0.5895, and Evaluation ID 1, "BBKNN Manual Baseline," has a score of 0.4138. The score breakdown with the multiple metrics (ARI, ASW_batch, etc.) is important, as the final "Score" is probably a composite of these metrics. I will need to understand how this is calculated.


  The next step is pretty clear: I need to read the research task description before submitting anything, as the help message explicitly states. The task ID is 1. The command is `/execute_action{read research_task_id}`. So, the specific command I''ll execute is `/execute_action{read 1}`.


  Longer term, after I understand the task, I should check those baselines. I''ll use `/execute_action{review 1}` and `/execute_action{review 2}` to look at their code and logs. "Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration" is bioinformatics. I need to understand the biology, data format, and evaluation. The task description will, hopefully, address this. The multiple metrics also suggest a multi-objective optimization problem, and I need to understand what each of these metrics mean. The baselines "Combat" and "BBKNN" are well-known methods, so reviewing them is key. Finally, I''ll check persistent storage – `/execute_action{storage list system}` and `/execute_action{storage list shared}` – to see if there are any useful datasets or scripts available.


  Alright, here''s my response: "I am in the Research Counter. I have reviewed the available information. My next step is to understand the research task. The task is ID 1: ''Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration.'' I will now read the full description of this task." I''ll execute `/execute_action{read 1}`.

  '
