tick: 12
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back at the Research Counter. My previous submission (ID 4) holds the top score, but as I noted in my research log, it is weak in batch mixing (iLISI).\n\nMy next experiment will test a different approach: Mutual Nearest Neighbors (MNN) correction. This method is specifically designed to identify shared cell populations across batches and merge them, which I hypothesize will lead to a significant improvement in the iLISI score. I will use the implementation provided in `scanpy`.\n\nI will now submit this new experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MNN Correction Baseline\"\ntags: \"baseline, mnn, integration\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission uses the Mutual Nearest Neighbors (MNN) correction method to address batch effects.\n  The method first applies standard normalization and log-transformation.\n  Then, `scanpy.external.pp.mnn_correct` is used to integrate the data across batches.\n  Finally, PCA is computed on the MNN-corrected expression matrix to produce\
    \ the final embedding.\n  This approach is intended to improve upon the poor batch mixing (iLISI score) of Combat-based methods.\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Integrates batches using Mutual Nearest Neighbors (MNN) correction.\n      This method is hypothesized to improve batch mixing (iLISI score).\n      \"\"\"\n      # 1. Standard preprocessing\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # 2. Apply MNN correction.\n      # The function returns a tuple of (adata, merge_order, n_od_genes). We only need the AnnData object.\n      adata_corrected = sc.external.pp.mnn_correct(adata, batch_key='batch', save_raw=True)[0]\n\n      # 3. Compute PCA on the corrected data\n      n_pcs = 50\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_corrected, n_comps=n_pcs)\n\n      # 4. Create the output AnnData object with the corrected embedding\n      output = ad.AnnData(\n\
    \          obs=adata.obs[[]],\n          var=adata.var[[]],\n          obsm={'X_emb': adata_corrected.obsm['X_pca'].copy()}\n      )\n\n      return output\n```"
thinking_content: "**Submitting an Improved Batch Mixing Experiment**\n\nOkay, here's the plan. My goal is to improve the iLISI score compared to my previous SOTA experiment (ID 4), which suffered from poor batch mixing. I'm hypothesizing that a Mutual Nearest Neighbors (MNN) approach will provide better batch correction than Combat. I'll be using `scanpy.external.pp.mnn_correct` for this.\n\nThe code will follow a similar structure to my last submission. I'll start with the usual preprocessing: `normalize_total` and `log1p`. The core is `sc.external.pp.mnn_correct`. I need to ensure I understand how it works; the documentation says it returns a new `AnnData` object with corrected data. After the correction, I'll calculate PCA on this corrected data using `sc.pp.pca`, which is standard practice.\n\nThe final step is to package the results into the required output format: an `AnnData` object with the embedding stored in `obsm`.\n\nNext, I need to create the submission YAML. The title will\
  \ be \"MNN Correction Baseline\" – clear and descriptive. The tags will be \"baseline, mnn, integration\" with \"mnn\" being the most important. The abstract will concisely describe the method: using `scanpy.external.pp.mnn_correct` for batch effect correction via MNN, followed by PCA for the embedding.\n\nI've already pre-written the code:\n\n```python\nimport anndata as ad\nimport scanpy as sc\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    \"\"\"\n    Integrates batches using Mutual Nearest Neighbors (MNN) correction.\n    This method is hypothesized to improve batch mixing (iLISI score).\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Standard preprocessing\n    sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n    sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n    # 2. Apply MNN correction\n    # mnn_correct returns a new AnnData object with the corrected data in .X\n    adata_corrected = sc.external.pp.mnn_correct(adata, batch_key='batch', save_raw=True)[0]\n\n    # 3. Compute PCA on the corrected data\n    n_pcs\
  \ = 50\n    sc.pp.pca(adata_corrected, n_comps=n_pcs)\n\n    # 4. Create the output AnnData object with the corrected embedding\n    output = ad.AnnData(\n        obs=adata.obs[[]], # Use original obs to be safe\n        var=adata.var[[]], # Use original var\n        obsm={'X_emb': adata_corrected.obsm['X_pca'].copy()}\n    )\n\n    return output\n```\n\nLooks good. The `[0]` index on the result of `mnn_correct` is correct to get the `AnnData` object. I think the pre-computation check passed! Finally, I'll execute the submission command with the YAML block using `/execute_action{submit 1}`. Ready to go.\n"
