  Analyzing my options, the Reflection Chamber is good for the long-term, but it might be too general at this point. Code organization, on the other hand, is a valuable, long-term investment, though it doesn''t do much for my current predicament. The BBKNN is good knowledge-wise. I can''t use public memory resources.


  Between the code organization and the BBKNN review, I think reviewing BBKNN is more pressing. My MNN experiment is running, so I should see the third available method and understand it. This will give me a third major approach to consider, after Combat and MNN. If MNN tanks, I''ll have a ready-made alternative to explore.


  My game plan is to stay put in the Research Counter and immediately review the "BBKNN Manual Baseline" (ID 1). I''ll scrutinize its strengths and weaknesses, especially compared to Combat and my MNN hypothesis. Then, once I have those insights and the results of ID 23, I''ll update my research log and map out the next experiment. A solid, scientific method! Now, time to execute the review!

  '
