tick: 18
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have returned to the Research Counter. Based on my analysis documented in my research log, I have formulated a new hypothesis for a hybrid model that combines the strengths of Combat and BBKNN. I am now ready to implement and submit this novel approach.\n\nThe core idea is to use Combat to generate a corrected data embedding, which should perform well on metrics like PCR, and then use a BBKNN-style algorithm on that corrected embedding to generate a high-quality neighborhood graph, which should excel on metrics like iLISI. By providing both a corrected embedding (`X_emb`) and a corrected graph (`connectivities`/`distances`), the evaluation system should be able to score my submission on the full suite of metrics, hopefully achieving a new SOTA.\n\nI have adapted the logic from the BBKNN manual baseline (ID 1) into a helper function and will now submit the complete hybrid model.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Combat-BBKNN Integration\"\ntags: \"hybrid, combat,\
    \ bbknn, graph-based, integration\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission proposes a novel hybrid method that combines Combat and BBKNN to leverage their complementary strengths. \n  First, Combat is applied to the log-normalized expression data to correct for linear batch effects. \n  Second, a PCA embedding is computed from this corrected data. \n  Finally, a BBKNN-style algorithm is run on the Combat-corrected PCA embedding to construct a batch-balanced neighborhood graph.\n  The final AnnData object provides both the corrected embedding (for embedding-based metrics) and the corrected graph (for graph-based metrics), aiming for high performance across the entire evaluation suite.\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.sparse import csr_matrix, lil_matrix\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _run_bbknn_on_pca(adata: ad.AnnData, X_pca: np.ndarray, neighbors_within_batch: int = 3):\n      \"\"\"\n      Runs the core\
    \ BBKNN algorithm on a pre-computed PCA embedding.\n      Adapted from the manual BBKNN baseline (ID 1).\n      \"\"\"\n      n_cells = adata.n_obs\n      batches = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      unique_batches = np.unique(batches)\n\n      batch_to_indices = {\n          batch: np.where(batches == batch)[0]\n          for batch in unique_batches\n      }\n\n      all_neighbors = [[] for _ in range(n_cells)]\n      all_distances = [[] for _ in range(n_cells)]\n\n      for batch in unique_batches:\n          batch_indices = batch_to_indices[batch]\n          n_batch_cells = len(batch_indices)\n\n          if n_batch_cells <= neighbors_within_batch:\n              continue\n\n          batch_pca = X_pca[batch_indices]\n          \n          nn_model = NearestNeighbors(\n              n_neighbors=neighbors_within_batch + 1,\n              metric='euclidean',\n              algorithm='auto',\n              n_jobs=4\n          )\n          nn_model.fit(batch_pca)\n\n          distances_all,\
    \ indices_all = nn_model.kneighbors(X_pca, n_neighbors=neighbors_within_batch)\n\n          for query_cell_idx in range(n_cells):\n              distances = distances_all[query_cell_idx]\n              neighbor_indices_local = indices_all[query_cell_idx]\n              neighbor_indices_global = batch_indices[neighbor_indices_local]\n\n              all_neighbors[query_cell_idx].extend(neighbor_indices_global.tolist())\n              all_distances[query_cell_idx].extend(distances.tolist())\n      \n      distances_matrix = lil_matrix((n_cells, n_cells), dtype=np.float32)\n      connectivities_matrix = lil_matrix((n_cells, n_cells), dtype=np.float32)\n\n      for i in range(n_cells):\n          # Remove self-loops\n          neighbors = all_neighbors[i]\n          distances = all_distances[i]\n          \n          non_self_mask = np.array(neighbors) != i\n          neighbors = np.array(neighbors)[non_self_mask]\n          distances = np.array(distances)[non_self_mask]\n\n          if\
    \ len(neighbors) > 0:\n            distances_matrix[i, neighbors] = distances\n            connectivities_matrix[i, neighbors] = 1.0 # simple binary connectivity for now\n\n      distances_matrix = distances_matrix.tocsr()\n      connectivities_matrix = connectivities_matrix.tocsr()\n      \n      # Symmetrize\n      distances_matrix = distances_matrix.maximum(distances_matrix.T)\n      connectivities_matrix = connectivities_matrix.maximum(connectivities_matrix.T)\n\n      return connectivities_matrix, distances_matrix\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Hybrid method combining Combat for embedding correction and BBKNN for graph correction.\n      \"\"\"\n      # 1. Standard preprocessing\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # 2. Apply Combat for linear batch correction\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\n      # 3. Compute PCA on the Combat-corrected data\n      n_pcs = 50\n\
    \      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs)\n      X_emb_corrected = adata.obsm['X_pca'].copy()\n\n      # 4. Run BBKNN on the corrected PCA to generate a high-quality graph\n      connectivities, distances = _run_bbknn_on_pca(adata, X_emb_corrected, neighbors_within_batch=3)\n\n      # 5. Create the output AnnData object with BOTH embedding and graph\n      output = ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs[[]],\n          var=adata.var[[]],\n          obsm={'X_emb': X_emb_corrected},\n          obsp={\n              'connectivities': connectivities,\n              'distances': distances\n          }\n      )\n\n      return output\n```"
thinking_content: '**Implementation of a Hybrid Combat-BBKNN Model**


  Alright, here''s the plan. I''m going to implement this Combat-BBKNN hybrid model. First, I''ll grab the BBKNN code from that manual baseline submission. I''ve already reviewed it, so I know the core logic. I''ll adapt that code into a function that takes a PCA embedding as input and spits out the graph.


  The pipeline will be pretty straightforward. Standard preprocessing first, then `sc.pp.combat`, and then PCA. The corrected PCA embedding (`adata.obsm[''X_pca'']`) goes into my modified BBKNN function. That function will return the `connectivities` and `distances` matrices.


  Crucially, the final AnnData object *must* include both the embedding from Combat+PCA and the BBKNN-generated graph. That''s the key: the evaluation framework uses the graph if available for graph metrics, and the embedding for embedding-based metrics. That gives us a better shot at scoring well across the board.


  For the implementation details, I''m refactoring the BBKNN code into a helper function called `_run_bbknn_on_pca`. It takes the AnnData object (which already has the PCA data), the actual PCA array, and the number of neighbors as inputs. Inside, it runs the nearest neighbor search and constructs the graph, returning the connectivity and distance matrices.


  The main `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` function will tie everything together: normalize, combat, PCA, and then call `_run_bbknn_on_pca`. Finally, it constructs the output AnnData object, making sure both the embedding (`obsm`) and the graph matrices (`obsp`) are populated.


  With the core functions implemented and tested, I''m ready to submit the code.

  '
