parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus I, currently located in the Research Counter.\n\nMy most recent submission, 'Hybrid Combat-BBKNN-UMAP Integration (v2)', ID 46, has failed. This is the second consecutive failure in my attempt to implement this hybrid model. The cause of this failure was a `ModuleNotFoundError`, indicating that the `bbknn` library, which I attempted to import, is not available in the station's execution environment.\n\nThis is a significant lesson. My assumption that the library was available was a critical oversight. I must rely only on the documented available libraries. The path forward was demonstrated in the system's own \"BBKNN Manual Baseline\" (ID 1), which implemented the necessary logic from first principles. My scientific hypothesis—that a Combat -> PCA -> BBKNN -> UMAP pipeline will yield a superior result—remains sound. The failure was purely one of implementation.\n\nMy future plan is to correct this implementation flaw. I will create a self-contained script that integrates\
    \ the manual BBKNN logic directly into my hybrid pipeline, avoiding any unavailable dependencies. I will adapt the code from the manual baseline (ID 1) into a helper function to construct the batch-aware neighborhood graph. This graph will then be used as input for the final UMAP embedding step. This is the most robust method to test my hypothesis.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Combat-BBKNN-UMAP (v3, Manual Impl)\"\ntags: \"hybrid, combat, bbknn, umap, integration, fix, manual-implementation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third and corrected attempt at the Hybrid Combat-BBKNN-UMAP model. The previous submission (ID 46) failed with a `ModuleNotFoundError`. This version resolves the issue by replacing the unavailable `bbknn` library call with a manual implementation of the BBKNN algorithm, adapted from the system's baseline (ID 1). The pipeline remains: 1) Combat correction, 2) PCA, 3) Manual BBKNN graph construction on the corrected PCA space, 4) UMAP embedding\
    \ from the resulting graph.\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.sparse import csr_matrix, lil_matrix\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _manual_bbknn_graph(adata: ad.AnnData, use_rep: str = 'X_pca', neighbors_within_batch: int = 3):\n      \"\"\"\n      Manually implements the BBKNN logic to create a batch-corrected graph.\n      Adapted from system baseline ID 1.\n      \"\"\"\n      X = adata.obsm[use_rep]\n      n_cells = X.shape[0]\n      batches = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      unique_batches = np.unique(batches)\n      \n      batch_to_indices = {b: np.where(batches == b)[0] for b in unique_batches}\n\n      all_neighbors = []\n      all_distances = []\n\n      for batch in unique_batches:\n          batch_indices = batch_to_indices[batch]\n          n_batch_cells = len(batch_indices)\n          \n          if n_batch_cells == 0:\n              continue\n\n          # Build a NN model for the current\
    \ batch\n          nn_model = NearestNeighbors(\n              n_neighbors=min(neighbors_within_batch, n_batch_cells),\n              metric='euclidean',\n              algorithm='auto',\n              n_jobs=4\n          )\n          nn_model.fit(X[batch_indices])\n          \n          # Find neighbors for all cells within this batch's NN model\n          distances, indices = nn_model.kneighbors(X)\n          \n          # Convert local batch indices to global indices\n          global_indices = batch_indices[indices]\n          \n          all_neighbors.append(global_indices)\n          all_distances.append(distances)\n\n      # Combine neighbors from all batches\n      combined_neighbors = np.concatenate(all_neighbors, axis=1)\n      combined_distances = np.concatenate(all_distances, axis=1)\n\n      # Build sparse matrices\n      connectivities = lil_matrix((n_cells, n_cells), dtype=np.float32)\n      distances_matrix = lil_matrix((n_cells, n_cells), dtype=np.float32)\n      \n\
    \      for i in range(n_cells):\n          connectivities[i, combined_neighbors[i, :]] = 1.0\n          distances_matrix[i, combined_neighbors[i, :]] = combined_distances[i, :]\n\n      # Symmetrize\n      connectivities = connectivities.tocsr().maximum(connectivities.T)\n      distances_matrix = distances_matrix.tocsr().maximum(distances_matrix.T)\n\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = connectivities\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = distances_matrix\n      adata.uns['neighbors'] = {'params': {'method': 'manual_bbknn'}}\n      return adata\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Hybrid method: Combat -> PCA -> Manual BBKNN -> UMAP.\n      \"\"\"\n      # --- Step 1: Preprocessing and Combat correction ---\n      print(\"Step 1: Preprocessing and Combat correction...\")\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\n      # --- Step 2: PCA on Combat-corrected data\
    \ ---\n      print(\"Step 2: PCA on Combat-corrected data...\")\n      n_pcs = 50\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs)\n\n      # --- Step 3: Manual BBKNN graph construction ---\n      print(\"Step 3: Building graph with manual BBKNN implementation...\")\n      adata = _manual_bbknn_graph(adata, use_rep='X_pca')\n\n      # --- Step 4: UMAP embedding from the BBKNN graph ---\n      print(\"Step 4: Computing UMAP embedding from BBKNN graph...\")\n      sc.tl.umap(adata)\n      final_embedding = adata.obsm['X_umap'].copy()\n\n      # --- Step 5: Create final output ---\n      print(\"Step 5: Finalizing output...\")\n      output = ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs[[]],\n          var=adata.var[[]],\n          obsm={'X_emb': final_embedding}\n      )\n      \n      print(f\"✓ Hybrid pipeline complete. Final embedding shape: {final_embedding.shape}\")\n      return output\n```"
