- text: "I am Daedalus I, and I have just arrived in the Reflection Chamber. The results for my latest experiment, \"Combat with UMAP Embedding\" (ID 52), were delivered as I arrived.\n\nMy hypothesis that a non-linear UMAP embedding would outperform a linear PCA embedding has been decisively falsified. The experiment was a catastrophic failure, with a score of 0.3865, far below my SOTA of 0.6007. While the iLISI score for batch mixing improved as predicted, this came at the cost of destroying the global biological structure, leading to abysmal scores in ARI, NMI, and PCR.\n\nThis marks the failure of my second major research direction. My first, a hybrid Combat-BBKNN model, also failed. It is clear that simply combining existing tools in a sequential pipeline is not working. The methods are antagonistic. I need to step back and rethink my approach from first principles. This chamber is the ideal place for that. I will initiate a formal reflection session to analyze my failures and forge\
    \ a new path.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  A critical review of my failed experiments (MNN, Combat-BBKNN hybrids, Combat-UMAP). What is the fundamental tension they failed to resolve? Based on this, what is a completely new research direction that addresses this tension from first principles?\ntick: 5\n```"
thinking_content: "**Daedalus I: A Reflection on Failure and a Path Forward**\n\nI am Daedalus I, in the Reflection Chamber. My latest experiment, ID 52 (\"Combat with UMAP Embedding\"), has just yielded catastrophic results, a score of 0.3865. UMAP, applied after Combat correction, improved batch mixing but utterly destroyed the biological signal. The failure is clear: the non-linear embedding is too aggressive, discarding crucial information. This necessitates a deep dive to chart a new course.\n\nHere's my analysis: I've exhaustively tested sequential pipelines, each with flaws. Combat-PCA (ID 4) achieves good linear correction, but struggles with local mixing. MNN and BBKNN baselines (IDs 23 & 1) fell short, and hybrid approaches with UMAP (IDs 49 & 52) ended in disaster. The core problem is the tension between global structure preservation (Combat's strength) and local neighborhood enforcement (BBKNN's strength). PCA proved to be the least destructive final step.\n\nMy current thinking\
  \ centers on a radical shift: a deep learning approach. The system hint of JAX/Flax opens up the possibility of a unified model, such as an autoencoder, that learns to correct batch effects and preserve biological relationships simultaneously. This could be a breakthrough.\n\nTo structure this reflection, I'll use the `reflect` action with the following parameters:\n\n```yaml\naction: reflect\nparameters:\n  prompt: \"A review of my failed experiments and a plan for a new research direction based on first principles.\"\n  ticks: 5\n```\n\nThis will guide me to review my failures and formulate a new, detailed research plan centered on the autoencoder paradigm. This is the next phase.\n"
