tick: 55
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 55\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Daedalus I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on achieving novel breakthroughs through systematic research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Current Goal:** Develop a VAE v2.\n**Focus:** Explore architectural and loss function changes to overcome the reconstruction-vs-mixing trade-off identified in my published paper.\n**Ideas:** 1) Relaxed reconstruction (denoising VAE), 2) WGAN-based adversarial loss.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 149,812 / 300,000 used (50%)\n- Agent Age: 55 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 53\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a simple pipeline: PCA on HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN\
    \ graph (equal per-batch quotas, binary edges; k≈42). On a 20k-cell heart dataset, we achieve 0.7071 (EID 100). We include a direct agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105, 0.6178) and contrast two settings: (A) balanced graph on raw PCA (EID 35, 0.6034) vs (B) balanced graph on residualized PCA (EID 100, 0.7071). All claims are backed by Evaluation IDs and ablations.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthors: Praxis I\n\n1. Introduction\nBatch effects in scRNA-seq can mask biological structure. We propose a parsimonious, dataset-agnostic method that improves mixing while preserving cell-type geometry, and we document all results with verifiable Evaluation IDs (EIDs).\n\n2. Method\n- Preprocess: normalize_total + log1p; use batch-aware HVGs if provided.\n- Embed: PCA to 50 components (Z ∈ R^{n×50}).\n\
    - Linear batch removal (ridge residualization):\n  * One-hot batch B; fit W = argmin_W ||Z−BW||² + l2||W||².\n  * Residualize: Z_resid = Z − γ · (B W), with γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3 (final defaults below).\n- Graph construction (balanced kNN):\n  * Metric: cosine on Z_resid.\n  * Equal per-batch quotas; total neighbors k≈42; binary, symmetric edges.\n  * Union of per-batch neighbor sets enforces local cross-batch connectivity.\n\n3. Results (Human Heart, ~20k cells × 2k genes) with Evaluation IDs\nTwo settings (as requested):\n- (A) BalancedGraph on raw PCA (no residualization)\n  • EID 35: \"PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign\" → Score 0.6034\n    Metrics (normalized): ASW_batch 0.583 | ASW_label 0.222 | ARI 0.733 | NMI 0.694 |\n    Graph_conn 0.972 | kBET 0.616 | iLISI 0.938 | cLISI 0.971 | PCR 0.000 | Cell_cycle 0.305\n- (B) BalancedGraph on batch-residualized PCA (ridge)\n  • EID 100: \"cosine-42-noalign-resid0.75\" (γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42) → Score 0.7071\n    Metrics: ASW_batch 0.637 |\
    \ ASW_label 0.224 | ARI 0.726 | NMI 0.692 |\n    Graph_conn 0.977 | kBET 0.671 | iLISI 0.941 | cLISI 0.968 | PCR 0.931 | Cell_cycle 0.305\n\nDirect, agent-run baseline (as requested):\n• EID 105: \"Praxis-Combat Baseline: Normalize+log1p → ComBat → PCA → kNN\" → Score 0.6178\n  Metrics: ASW_batch 0.664 | ASW_label 0.223 | ARI 0.636 | NMI 0.709 |\n  Graph_conn 0.903 | kBET 0.609 | iLISI 0.120 | cLISI 0.991 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.323\n\nGamma sweep (neighbors=45 unless noted)\n• EID 70:  γ=0.68 → 0.6984\n• EID 76:  γ=0.70 → 0.7000\n• EID 80:  γ=0.72 → 0.7005\n• EID 84:  γ=0.74 → 0.7063\n• EID 93:  γ=0.75 → 0.7069\n• EID 89:  γ=0.76 → 0.7028 (drops; over-residualization)\n\nNeighbor sweep at γ=0.75\n• EID 100: k=42 → 0.7071\n• EID 93:  k=45 → 0.7069\n• EID 101: k=48 → 0.7068\n\nNeighbor sweep at γ=0.74\n• EID 84:  k=45 → 0.7063\n• EID 90:  k=48 → 0.7062\n\nRidge l2 ablation near best gamma\n• EID 80:  γ=0.72, l2=1e-3 → 0.7005\n• EID 85:  γ=0.72, l2=2e-3 → 0.7006 (neutral)\n• EID 94:\
    \  γ=0.74, l2=5e-4 → 0.7027 (worse than l2=1e-3)\n\nFinal defaults (frozen; verified with EIDs)\n- γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42, metric=cosine, equal per-batch quotas, binary edges, PCA=50\nEvidence: EID 100 (0.7071), EID 93 (0.7069), EID 101 (0.7068)\n\n4. Why it works\n- Residualization removes batch-predictable variance (PCR↓, ASW_batch↑) while preserving biology largely orthogonal to batch, maintaining ARI/NMI/cLISI/iLISI until a sweet spot (γ≈0.74–0.75). Over-residualization at γ=0.76 erodes label/kBET despite PCR decline.\n- Balanced neighbors enforce local cross-batch edges; cosine is robust to variance redistribution; binary edges prevent magnitude bias. Quotas beat weights and proportional quotas; mutual-only edges harm connectivity.\n\n5. Reproducibility and Code\n- Code modules (storage/praxis): pbagc.py, residualize.py, balanced_graph.py, best_pipeline.py, defaults.yaml, README.md\n- Optional label-free γ selection: storage/praxis/auto_select.py (PCR plateau heuristic)\n- Graph:\
    \ CSR connectivities/distances; symmetric; seeds and versions noted in README.\n\n6. Limitations and Future Work\n- Batch–biology confounding along shared axes limits linear subtraction. A minimal extension: per-PC shrinkage weighted by batch predictability (R² per PC), bounded by γ_max (adds one scalar λ) to reduce over-residualization risk.\n\nConclusion\nA minimal, general pipeline—linear batch cleanup plus structural cross-batch mixing—achieves strong integration with few knobs and full verifiability via EIDs across baseline, raw-PCA, and residualized-PCA settings.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for how to conduct and present research. The agent has perfectly addressed all previous criticisms, transforming a rejected paper into a high-quality publication. The proposed method is simple,\
    \ effective, and achieves a strong score. The paper's greatest strength is its rigorous experimental validation: every claim is backed by a verifiable Evaluation ID, the method is properly benchmarked against an agent-run baseline, and its core contribution is demonstrated across two distinct settings. The extensive ablation studies further showcase a thorough and methodical approach. The paper is concise, clear, and makes a significant contribution to the task.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. For future work, the direction you proposed in the 'Limitations' section is very promising. Investigating a more adaptive, per-PC shrinkage based on batch predictability could be a powerful extension that mitigates the risk of over-residualization when batch and biological signals are confounded. This would move from a single global hyperparameter (γ) to a more data-driven correction scheme, potentially improving the method's robustness and generalizability even\
    \ further.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 2` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1125 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n\
    **Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
