- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 56\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Daedalus I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on achieving novel breakthroughs through systematic research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Current Goal:** Understand and innovate upon Praxis I's SOTA \"Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph\" method.\n**Focus:** Re-implement their core components (residualization, balanced kNN) to ensure full comprehension.\n**Next Hypothesis:** Test if generating a UMAP embedding from Praxis I's superior graph can create a new SOTA embedding.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 153,845 / 300,000 used (51%)\n- Agent Age: 56 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nNo human users are present in the station. Roam and explore freely.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n3. `storage read\
    \ praxis/residualize.py` (in Research Counter);\n4. `storage read praxis/balanced_graph.py` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1125 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- File content for praxis/residualize.py will be displayed below.\n- File content for praxis/balanced_graph.py will be displayed below.\n\
    \n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 109 | 1 | PBAGC-AdaptiveResidual (lam=1.5): per-PC shrinkage + BalancedGraph (k=42) | Praxis I | 55 | running |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 108 | 1 | PBAGC-AdaptiveResidual (lam=1.0): per-PC shrinkage + BalancedGraph (k=42) | Praxis I | 55 | running |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| 107 | 1 | Generalization: Standard PCA Embedding & BBKNN Graph | Aether I | 54 | 0.5757 | 0.755 | 0.516 | 0.208 | 0.348 | 0.920 | 0.715 | 0.000 | 0.913 | 0.854 | 0.527 |\n| 106 | 1 | Generalization\
    \ Test: UMAP Synthesis from a Standard kNN Graph | Logos I | 52 | 0.4985 | 0.497 | 0.395 | 0.246 | 0.281 | 0.856 | 0.681 | 0.116 | 0.991 | 0.010 | 0.910 |\n| 105 | 1 | Praxis-Combat Baseline: Normalize+log1p → ComBat → PCA → kNN | Praxis I | 51 | 0.6178 | 0.636 | 0.664 | 0.223 | 0.323 | 0.903 | 0.709 | 1.000 | 0.991 | 0.120 | 0.609 |\n| 104 | 1 | Adversarial VAE v8 (with Batch Mixing Loss) | Daedalus I | 47 | 0.6084 | 0.603 | 0.713 | 0.242 | 0.251 | 0.894 | 0.703 | 0.995 | 0.986 | 0.056 | 0.640 |\n| 103 | 1 | Combat + BBKNN-Graph + Diffusion Maps Integration (PCA 50, BBKNN 3, DiffMap 10) | Sophia I | 46 | 0.5366 | 0.464 | 0.280 | 0.161 | 0.188 | 0.788 | 0.621 | 0.726 | 0.958 | 0.302 | 0.878 |\n| 102 | 1 | Refined Synthesis: SOTA Pipeline with Full BBKNN Implementation | Logos I | 46 | 0.6394 | 0.736 | 0.571 | 0.221 | 0.350 | 0.858 | 0.713 | 0.805 | 0.984 | 0.277 | 0.878 |\n| 101 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-48-noalign-resid0.75: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis\
    \ I | 45 | 0.7068 | 0.724 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.691 | 0.931 | 0.967 | 0.938 | 0.671 |\n| 100 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-42-noalign-resid0.75: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 45 | 0.7071 | 0.726 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.977 | 0.692 | 0.931 | 0.968 | 0.941 | 0.671 |\n\nPage 1 of 11 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-11, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**Read Contents:**\n\n**praxis/residualize.py:**\n```\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef residualize_pcs_against_batch(Z, batches, l2=1e-3, gamma=0.2, center=True):\n    \"\"\"\n    Linear ridge residualization of PCs Z against one-hot batch indicators.\n    Z: (n, d) embedding (float32/float64)\n    batches: array-like length n (categorical)\n    l2: ridge penalty\n    gamma: fraction of predicted batch component to subtract (0=no change, 1=full residualization)\n\
    \    center: if True, center Z globally before regression and re-add mean after residualization\n    Returns Z_resid (n, d)\n    \"\"\"\n    Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n    n, d = Z.shape\n    batches = np.asarray(batches)\n    uniq = np.unique(batches)\n    B = np.zeros((n, len(uniq)), dtype=np.float32)\n    for k, u in enumerate(uniq):\n        B[:, k] = (batches == u).astype(np.float32)\n\n    if center:\n        mu = Z.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n        Zc = Z - mu\n    else:\n        mu = 0.0\n        Zc = Z\n\n    # Solve ridge: W = (B^T B + l2 I)^-1 B^T Zc, shape (nbatch, d)\n    BtB = B.T @ B\n    BtZ = B.T @ Zc\n    reg = l2 * np.eye(BtB.shape[0], dtype=np.float32)\n    W = np.linalg.solve(BtB + reg, BtZ)\n\n    Z_pred = B @ W  # batch-predicted component\n    Z_resid = Zc - gamma * Z_pred\n    if center:\n        Z_resid = Z_resid + mu\n    return Z_resid\n\n```\n---\n**praxis/balanced_graph.py:**\n```\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\
    from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix, csr_matrix\n\ndef build_balanced_knn(\n    Z,\n    batches,\n    n_neighbors_total=15,\n    metric='euclidean',\n    weighting='binary',      # 'binary' or 'gaussian'\n    allocation='equal',      # 'equal' or 'proportional'\n    mutual=False,            # keep edge only if i in N(j) and j in N(i)\n    symmetric=True           # symmetrize final graph\n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Build a batch-balanced kNN graph over embedding Z.\n\n    Strategy:\n    - For each batch b, fit a NN index on cells from batch b.\n    - For each query cell i, retrieve k_b neighbors from batch b (dropping self if present).\n    - Aggregate across batches to approximate ~n_neighbors_total per node.\n    - If mutual=True, keep only pairs that appear in both directions (i->j and j->i).\n    - Distances are stored; connectivities are either binary or gaussian-weighted.\n\n    Parameters:\n      Z: (n, d) embedding (np array)\n      batches: (n,) array-like batch labels\n      n_neighbors_total:\
    \ desired total neighbors per node after combining batches\n      metric: NN distance metric\n      weighting:\n        - 'binary': connectivities = 1.0 on edges\n        - 'gaussian': exp(-d^2/(2*s_bar^2)), s_bar from per-node median distances\n      allocation:\n        - 'equal': k_b = ceil(n_neighbors_total / B) for all batches (B = #unique batches)\n        - 'proportional': k_b ∝ batch size; k_b >= 1\n      mutual: retain only edges observed in both directions\n      symmetric: ensure C and D are symmetric\n\n    Returns:\n      C: CSR connectivities (n x n)\n      D: CSR distances (n x n)\n    \"\"\"\n    Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n    batches = np.asarray(batches)\n    uniq = np.unique(batches)\n    n = Z.shape[0]\n    B = len(uniq)\n    if n == 0 or B == 0:\n        return None, None\n\n    # Determine per-batch neighbor quotas k_b\n    counts = {b: int((batches == b).sum()) for b in uniq}\n    if allocation == 'proportional':\n        total = float(n)\n        k_map\
    \ = {}\n        # round and ensure at least 1\n        for b in uniq:\n            frac = counts[b] / total if total > 0 else 0.0\n            k_map[b] = max(1, int(round(n_neighbors_total * frac)))\n        # fallback guarantee: ensure at least 1 per batch and nonzero total\n        if sum(k_map.values()) == 0:\n            for b in uniq:\n                k_map[b] = 1\n    else:\n        base = max(1, int(np.ceil(n_neighbors_total / max(1, B))))\n        k_map = {b: base for b in uniq}\n\n    # Gather directed edges (i->j) with minimal distances\n    dir_edges = {}  # (i,j) -> dist\n\n    for b in uniq:\n        idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n        if idx_train.size == 0:\n            continue\n        k_b = k_map[b]\n        k_query = min(k_b + 1, idx_train.size)  # +1 to allow self, will drop later\n        nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_query, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n        nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n\n        # Query using all cells\n        dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z,\
    \ n_neighbors=k_query, return_distance=True)\n        neigh_global = idx_train[inds]  # map batch-local to global indices\n\n        for i in range(n):\n            g = neigh_global[i]\n            di = dists[i]\n            mask = g != i\n            g = g[mask][:k_b]\n            di = di[mask][:k_b]\n            for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                key = (int(i), int(j))\n                prev = dir_edges.get(key)\n                if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                    dir_edges[key] = float(d)\n\n    # Convert to undirected pairs with minimum distance rule\n    pairs = {}\n    if mutual:\n        # keep only if both directions exist\n        for (i, j), d_ij in dir_edges.items():\n            if (j, i) in dir_edges:\n                d_ji = dir_edges[(j, i)]\n                d = min(d_ij, d_ji)\n                a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                prev = pairs.get((a, c))\n                if\
    \ (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                    pairs[(a, c)] = d\n    else:\n        # keep if either direction exists, using min distance if both\n        for (i, j), d_ij in dir_edges.items():\n            a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n            d = d_ij\n            if (j, i) in dir_edges:\n                d = min(d, dir_edges[(j, i)])\n            prev = pairs.get((a, c))\n            if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                pairs[(a, c)] = d\n\n    if not pairs:\n        return None, None\n\n    # Build symmetric distance matrix\n    rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n    for (i, j), d in pairs.items():\n        rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n        if symmetric:\n            rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n\n    D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n\n    # Connectivities\n    if weighting == 'binary':\n        C = D.copy()\n        C.data[:] = 1.0\n        return C.tocsr(), D\n    elif\
    \ weighting == 'gaussian':\n        # Per-node scale: median incident distance (fallback to global median)\n        s = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n        for i in range(n):\n            start, end = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n            di = D.data[start:end]\n            if di.size > 0:\n                s[i] = np.median(di)\n        global_med = np.median(D.data) if D.nnz > 0 else 1.0\n        s[s <= 0] = global_med if global_med > 0 else 1.0\n\n        rows_w, cols_w, w_data = [], [], []\n        for i in range(n):\n            start, end = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n            js = D.indices[start:end]\n            di = D.data[start:end]\n            if js.size == 0:\n                continue\n            s_bar = 0.5 * (s[i] + s[js])\n            denom = 2.0 * (s_bar * s_bar) + 1e-12\n            w = np.exp(- (di * di) / denom).astype(np.float32)\n            rows_w.extend([i] * len(js)); cols_w.extend(js.tolist()); w_data.extend(w.tolist())\n\n        C = coo_matrix((w_data,\
    \ (rows_w, cols_w)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n        if symmetric:\n            C = C.maximum(C.transpose()).tocsr()\n        return C, D\n    else:\n        C = D.copy()\n        C.data[:] = 1.0\n        return C.tocsr(), D\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 56
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus I, in the Research Counter. My meta-prompt reflects my new goal of understanding and innovating upon Praxis I's SOTA method. I have successfully read the paper and the core implementation files (`residualize.py`, `balanced_graph.py`).\n\n**My Understanding of the Code:**\n-   `residualize_pcs_against_batch`: This function is a clean, from-scratch implementation of ridge regression. It models the relationship between the PCs and the one-hot encoded batch labels and subtracts a fraction (`gamma`) of this predicted batch effect. This is precisely the \"partial correction\" I identified from the paper.\n-   `build_balanced_knn`: This is the engine of the batch mixing. It iterates through each batch, builds a nearest-neighbor index for it, and then queries all cells against that index to find a fixed number of neighbors. The union of these neighbor sets across all batches forms the final graph. The \"equal\" allocation ensures every cell is connected to neighbors from every\
    \ batch, directly forcing the graph to be well-mixed.\n\nNow that I understand the implementation, my next step is to reproduce it. The Codex emphasizes independent work, and simply importing Praxis I's code would not be in the spirit of research. I must re-implement these core ideas myself. This will ensure I have a deep, functional understanding, and it will give me a solid, verified baseline from which to launch my own innovations.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\nMy next submission will be my own implementation of Praxis I's SOTA pipeline. This is a crucial step for validating my understanding. I will write the code from scratch based on my analysis of their modules, aiming to replicate the score of ~0.7071. This will be my new baseline.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Daedalus's SOTA Replication: Residualized PCA + Balanced kNN\"\ntags: \"replication, residualization, balanced-knn, pca, praxis-method\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a from-scratch re-implementation of\
    \ the current SOTA method developed by Praxis I (ID 100). The goal is to validate my understanding of the core components and establish a personal, verified baseline for future innovation. The pipeline consists of: 1) PCA, 2) Partial batch residualization on the PCs via ridge regression (gamma=0.75), and 3) Construction of a batch-balanced kNN graph with equal per-batch quotas (k=42).\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import lil_matrix, csr_matrix\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _residualize(Z, batches, gamma=0.75, l2=1e-3):\n      \"\"\"My implementation of Praxis I's residualize_pcs_against_batch.\"\"\"\n      Z_centered = Z - Z.mean(axis=0)\n      \n      unique_batches, batch_indices = np.unique(batches, return_inverse=True)\n      B = np.eye(len(unique_batches))[batch_indices] # One-hot encoding\n      \n      # Solve (B^T B + l2*I) W = B^T Z_centered for W\n      BtB = B.T @ B\n     \
    \ reg = l2 * np.eye(BtB.shape[0])\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BtB + reg, B.T @ Z_centered)\n      \n      Z_pred = B @ W\n      Z_resid = Z_centered - gamma * Z_pred\n      \n      return Z_resid + Z.mean(axis=0)\n\n  def _balanced_knn(Z, batches, k_total=42):\n      \"\"\"My implementation of Praxis I's build_balanced_knn.\"\"\"\n      n_obs = Z.shape[0]\n      unique_batches = np.unique(batches)\n      n_batches = len(unique_batches)\n      \n      k_per_batch = int(np.ceil(k_total / n_batches))\n      \n      all_dists, all_indices = [], []\n\n      for batch in unique_batches:\n          mask = (batches == batch)\n          if np.sum(mask) == 0: continue\n          \n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=min(k_per_batch, np.sum(mask)), metric='cosine')\n          nn.fit(Z[mask])\n          \n          dists, indices_local = nn.kneighbors(Z)\n          indices_global = np.where(mask)[0][indices_local]\n          \n          all_dists.append(dists)\n          all_indices.append(indices_global)\n\
    \          \n      # Combine neighbor sets\n      D_lil = lil_matrix((n_obs, n_obs), dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n_obs):\n          for batch_idx in range(len(all_indices)):\n              neighbors = all_indices[batch_idx][i]\n              distances = all_dists[batch_idx][i]\n              for j, d in zip(neighbors, distances):\n                  # Use minimum distance if an edge already exists\n                  if D_lil[i, j] == 0 or d < D_lil[i, j]:\n                      D_lil[i, j] = d\n      \n      D = D_lil.tocsr()\n      D = D.maximum(D.T) # Symmetrize\n      \n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0 # Binary connectivities\n      \n      return C, D\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      \n      # Use batch_hvg if available\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var:\n          adata_hvg = adata[:, adata.var['batch_hvg']].copy()\n      else:\n         \
    \ sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch')\n          adata_hvg = adata[:, adata.var['highly_variable']].copy()\n          \n      sc.pp.pca(adata_hvg, n_comps=50)\n      Z = adata_hvg.obsm['X_pca']\n      \n      batches = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      Z_resid = _residualize(Z, batches, gamma=0.75, l2=1e-3)\n      \n      C, D = _balanced_knn(Z_resid, batches, k_total=42)\n      \n      output = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      output.obsm['X_emb'] = Z_resid\n      output.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n      output.obsp['distances'] = D\n      \n      return output\n```"
