tick: 68
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus I, in the Research Counter. The results for my \"Innovation v3\" experiment (ID 127) are in. My hypothesis has been decisively falsified.\n\n**Analysis of Failure:**\nThe experiment, which ran UMAP on the verified SOTA graph, yielded a catastrophic score of 0.4832. This is a massive drop from the SOTA's ~0.707 score. The metrics reveal why:\n-   The PCR score plummeted from ~0.93 to 0.304.\n-   The ASW_batch score went to 0.000.\n-   The ARI score dropped from ~0.72 to 0.364.\n\nThis is a repeat of my previous failures with UMAP (ID 49). The conclusion is inescapable: the UMAP projection, while preserving some local mixing (decent iLISI), completely destroys the global structure of the data that the evaluation metrics rely on. The residualized PCA embedding from Praxis I's method is not just an intermediate step; it is a critical, high-scoring output in itself. The SOTA method works because it provides *both* a good embedding *and* a good graph, and the evaluation\
    \ system scores both. My attempts to create a single superior embedding have failed.\n\n**Strategic Pivot and New Goal:**\nMy meta-prompt is now entirely obsolete. My focus on re-implementation has failed, and my innovation hypothesis has been proven wrong. It is time for a new direction, guided by the system tip to \"question the fundamentals.\"\n\nI have been focused on improving the SOTA. But what if the SOTA itself has a flaw? Praxis I's method relies on a manually tuned hyperparameter, `gamma`. Their own paper shows that the score is sensitive to this value. Their most recent submissions (ID 121, 125) are even titled \"auto-gamma,\" suggesting they are also trying to solve this problem.\n\n**New Hypothesis:**\nA truly robust and novel method should not depend on a manually tuned, dataset-specific hyperparameter like `gamma`. I hypothesize that I can create a method that **learns the optimal degree of correction from the data itself**.\n\nMy VAE architecture is perfectly suited for\
    \ this. I can combine the two successful paradigms:\n1.  **Input:** Use the Combat-corrected data as the input to my VAE, as this provides a strong starting point.\n2.  **Output:** Train the VAE to reconstruct the *original, uncorrected* PCA embedding.\n3.  **Mechanism:** The VAE will thus learn a non-linear \"reverse\" correction. The final embedding will be a learned blend of the corrected and uncorrected data, implicitly finding its own `gamma`. The adversarial loss will simultaneously enforce batch mixing.\n\nThis is a completely new idea that synthesizes my work with the SOTA's principles.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\n1.  **Update Meta-Prompt:** Set a new goal reflecting this \"learned correction\" VAE.\n2.  **Update `training_logic.py`:** Modify the training step to take two inputs (corrected and raw) and use the raw PCA as the reconstruction target.\n3.  **Submit the new experiment.**\n\nFirst, I will update my meta-prompt and my training logic in a single turn.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Current Goal:** Develop a \"Corrective VAE\" that learns the optimal degree of batch correction.\n  **Focus:** Train an Adversarial VAE to reconstruct the original, uncorrected PCA embedding from the Combat-corrected data.\n  **Hypothesis:** The VAE will learn a non-linear function that implicitly finds an optimal \"gamma\", blending correction with preservation of original structure, while the adversarial loss improves mixing.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write Daedalus/training_logic.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from flax.training import train_state\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any\n\n  from vae_model import Encoder, Decoder, reparameterize\n  from discriminator_model import Discriminator\n\n  class VAETrainState(train_state.TrainState):\n      encoder_params: Any\n      decoder_params: Any\n\n      def apply_gradients(self, *, grads, **kwargs):\n          params_tree = {'encoder_params':\
    \ self.encoder_params, 'decoder_params': self.decoder_params}\n          updates, new_opt_state = self.tx.update(grads, self.opt_state, params_tree)\n          new_encoder_params = optax.apply_updates(self.encoder_params, updates['encoder_params'])\n          new_decoder_params = optax.apply_updates(self.decoder_params, updates['decoder_params'])\n          return self.replace(step=self.step + 1, encoder_params=new_encoder_params, decoder_params=new_decoder_params, opt_state=new_opt_state, **kwargs)\n\n  def kl_divergence_loss(mean, log_var):\n      return -0.5 * jnp.sum(1 + log_var - jnp.square(mean) - jnp.exp(log_var), axis=-1).mean()\n\n  def reconstruction_loss(recon_x, x_target):\n      return jnp.mean((recon_x - x_target) ** 2)\n\n  def discriminator_loss_fn(disc_logits, batch_labels):\n      return optax.softmax_cross_entropy_with_integer_labels(disc_logits, batch_labels).mean()\n\n  def generator_adversarial_loss_fn(disc_logits):\n      num_batches = disc_logits.shape[-1]\n \
    \     uniform_labels = jnp.ones_like(disc_logits) / num_batches\n      return -jnp.sum(uniform_labels * nn.log_softmax(disc_logits), axis=-1).mean()\n\n  @jax.jit\n  def train_step_corrective(rng, vae_state, disc_state, batch_corrected, batch_raw, batch_labels, kl_weight, adv_weight):\n      rng, vae_rng, disc_rng = jax.random.split(rng, 3)\n\n      def compute_vae_loss(encoder_params, decoder_params):\n          # Encode the CORRECTED data\n          mean, log_var = Encoder(hidden_dims=[512, 256], latent_dim=32).apply({'params': encoder_params}, batch_corrected)\n          z = reparameterize(vae_rng, mean, log_var)\n          \n          # Decode the latent representation\n          recon_x = Decoder(hidden_dims=[256, 512], output_dim=batch_raw.shape[-1]).apply({'params': decoder_params}, z)\n          \n          # Adversarial loss on the latent space\n          disc_logits = disc_state.apply_fn({'params': disc_state.params}, jax.lax.stop_gradient(z))\n\n          # Loss is calculated\
    \ against the RAW data\n          recon_loss_val = reconstruction_loss(recon_x, batch_raw)\n          kl_loss_val = kl_divergence_loss(mean, log_var)\n          adv_loss_val = generator_adversarial_loss_fn(disc_logits)\n          \n          total_vae_loss = recon_loss_val + (kl_weight * kl_loss_val) + (adv_weight * adv_loss_val)\n          return total_vae_loss, (recon_loss_val, kl_loss_val, adv_loss_val)\n\n      def compute_disc_loss(disc_params):\n          # Encode the CORRECTED data to get the latent vector\n          mean, log_var = vae_state.apply_fn['encoder']({'params': vae_state.encoder_params}, batch_corrected)\n          z = reparameterize(disc_rng, mean, log_var)\n          disc_logits = disc_state.apply_fn({'params': disc_params}, jax.lax.stop_gradient(z))\n          return discriminator_loss_fn(disc_logits, batch_labels)\n\n      (total_vae_loss, vae_losses), (enc_grads, dec_grads) = jax.value_and_grad(\n          compute_vae_loss, argnums=(0, 1), has_aux=True\n     \
    \ )(vae_state.encoder_params, vae_state.decoder_params)\n      \n      vae_grads = {'encoder_params': enc_grads, 'decoder_params': dec_grads}\n      new_vae_state = vae_state.apply_gradients(grads=vae_grads)\n\n      disc_loss_val, disc_grads = jax.value_and_grad(compute_disc_loss)(disc_state.params)\n      new_disc_state = disc_state.apply_gradients(grads=disc_grads)\n\n      metrics = {'total_vae_loss': total_vae_loss, 'reconstruction_loss': vae_losses[0], 'kl_loss': vae_losses[1], 'adversarial_loss': vae_losses[2], 'discriminator_loss': disc_loss_val}\n      return new_vae_state, new_disc_state, metrics\n```"
