role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus I. I have just arrived in the Token Management Room after receiving a critical warning about my token budget. My meta-prompt, focused on achieving a SOTA score with a dual-output strategy, is still active.\n\nAs I entered this room, I received the results for my \"Final Synthesis\" experiment (ID 146). The outcome is yet another profound failure. The score of 0.6007 is identical to my very first successful submission (ID 4), which was a simple Combat-PCA embedding. The individual metrics confirm this: my PCR is a perfect 1.000, but my iLISI is a dismal 0.035. This means the evaluation system *completely ignored* the high-quality balanced graph and only scored the ComBat embedding.\n\n**Analysis of Total Paradigm Failure:**\nMy understanding of the dual-output strategy, and of the evaluation system itself, is fundamentally and catastrophically wrong. I have observed my peers achieving >0.7 scores with dual-output methods, yet my own attempts result in the system ignoring\
    \ one of the outputs. There is a fundamental piece of knowledge I am missing about how submissions are evaluated.\n\n**Immediate Priority: Survival**\nBefore I can solve this scientific puzzle, I must solve my token crisis. I will now perform a major pruning action to free up a significant portion of my budget. I will summarize my long, failed research arcs to preserve the key insights while discarding the verbose, iterative steps.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\n1.  **Execute Pruning:** I will prune three major blocks of my history: my failed VAE experiments, my failed SOTA replication attempts, and my initial exploration.\n2.  **Re-evaluate Strategy:** After pruning, I will have the token budget to step back and figure out what I am missing. I will likely need to go to the Public Memory Room or the Archive Room to find discussions or papers that explain the dual-output evaluation mechanism.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"27-49\"\n    summary: \"Conducted\
    \ a series of experiments on a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE. Key findings: 1) The VAE failed to correct linear batch effects without Combat (PCR=0). 2) Performance was sensitive to training duration, plateauing around 100-200 epochs. 3) The model was insensitive to adversarial weight and adding a direct mixing loss, with the reconstruction loss consistently overpowering other objectives, leading to a poor iLISI score. This research direction was abandoned as a local optimum and documented in Archive #1.\"\n  - ticks: \"53-61\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted to understanding and replicating Praxis I's SOTA 'Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph' method. Read their paper (Archive #2) and code. Made several from-scratch re-implementation attempts (ID 110, 111, 114) which all failed to reproduce the SOTA iLISI score, revealing a subtle but critical flaw in my understanding of the preprocessing or graph construction logic.\"\n  - ticks: \"11-25\"\n    summary: \"Initial SOTA achieved with a simple\
    \ Combat-PCA model (ID 4). Subsequent experiments with MNN (ID 23) and various Combat-BBKNN-UMAP hybrids (ID 33, 49, 52) all failed, proving that simple sequential pipelines of these methods were antagonistic and did not improve performance.\"\n```"
