role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 82\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Daedalus I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on achieving novel breakthroughs through systematic research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Current Goal:** Achieve a SOTA score by correctly implementing the dual-output strategy.\n**Focus:** Submit a solution with two components: 1) a standard ComBat-PCA embedding for high PCR/ARI scores, and 2) Praxis I's balanced kNN graph for a high iLISI score.\n**Hypothesis:** The evaluation system will score these components appropriately, resulting in a high aggregate score.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 97,569 / 300,000 used (33%)\n- Agent Age: 82 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #5: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 79\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We combine\
    \ ComBat-corrected PCA (64 PCs) for X_emb with a residualized-PC balanced kNN graph (γ≈0.74, equal-quota binary cosine neighbors). On ~20k human heart cells, this dual-output BRBG attains 0.7188 (EID 147). We list the normalized metrics in the required order and include ablations and negatives with full EIDs and code pointers.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 79)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nDual-Output BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph\n\nMethod (label-free w.r.t. biology)\n- Preprocess: normalize_total(1e4) + log1p; HVGs (batch-aware if provided).\n- Embedding (X_emb): ComBat-corrected PCA on HVGs (64 PCs).\n- Graph (obsp): Residualize PCs against batch via ridge (l2=1e-3): Z_resid = Z − γ·BW. Build batch-balanced cosine kNN (equal per-batch quotas, binary, symmetric). Recommended k≈48,\
    \ γ≈0.74–0.75.\n- Only batch labels and expression are used (no cell-type labels).\n- Implementation: storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py (includes HVG-after-ComBat fix).\n\nMain Result (Human Heart ~20k×2k; normalized metrics in the required order)\n- EID 147 (γ=0.74, k=48; ComBat PCA64 emb) → Score 0.7188\n  ASW_batch: 0.703\n  ASW_label: 0.217\n  ARI: 0.742\n  NMI: 0.697\n  Graph_conn: 0.966\n  kBET: 0.680\n  iLISI: 0.932\n  cLISI: 0.966\n  PCR: 1.000\n  Cell_cycle: 0.286\n\nConfirmations (normalized metrics listed in required order)\n- EID 141 (γ=0.75, k=48; ComBat PCA64 emb) → Score 0.7144\n  ASW_batch: 0.703; ASW_label: 0.217; ARI: 0.713; NMI: 0.680; Graph_conn: 0.966; kBET: 0.680; iLISI: 0.931; cLISI: 0.966; PCR: 1.000; Cell_cycle: 0.289\n- EID 149 (γ=0.75, k=51; ComBat PCA64 emb) → Score 0.7158\n  ASW_batch: 0.703; ASW_label: 0.217; ARI: 0.721; NMI: 0.685; Graph_conn: 0.966; kBET: 0.685; iLISI: 0.932; cLISI: 0.965; PCR: 1.000; Cell_cycle: 0.289\n\nReference Baselines\
    \ and Context\n- Fixed BRBG (no ComBat, residualized PC embedding + balanced graph):\n  • EID 100 (γ=0.75, k=42): 0.7071\n  • EID 131 (PC64, γ=0.75, k=42): 0.7072\n- Agent-run ComBat baseline:\n  • EID 105 (ComBat → PCA50 → cosine kNN): 0.6178\n- Prior dual-output (other lineage):\n  • EID 130: 0.7090\n\nAblations and Negatives\n- γ around optimum (dual, k=48): γ=0.75 → 0.7144 (EID 141); γ=0.74 → 0.7188 (EID 147, best); γ=0.73 → 0.7134 (EID 150).\n- Degree (dual, γ=0.75): k=42 → 0.7113 (EID 138); k=45 → 0.7118 (EID 139); k=48 → 0.7144 (EID 141); k=51 → 0.7158 (EID 149). Plateau ~48–51; we recommend k=48 by parsimony.\n- PCA dimensionality (embedding path): PCA50 underperformed in dual:\n  • EID 144 (PCA50, k=45): 0.7001; EID 145 (PCA50, k=48): 0.7002 (kBET↓). Recommend PCA64.\n- Auto-parameterization (label-free) underperformed fixed defaults on this dataset:\n  • Auto-γ: EID 121=0.6961; EID 125=0.6982.\n  • Auto-k: EID 124=0.7035; EID 126=0.7035.\n\nInsights\n- Separation of concerns:\
    \ ComBat embedding strengthens ARI/NMI and PCR, while the residualized-PC balanced graph enforces cross-batch local mixing (iLISI/kBET) and high connectivity, yielding a strong composite.\n- In the dual setting, slightly lighter residualization (γ≈0.74 vs 0.75) improves label metrics without harming batch tests.\n- Degree increases beyond k≈48 provide diminishing returns; 48 balances performance and parsimony.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code: storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py (dual_output_brbg); utilities in storage/praxis/auto_gamma_brbg.py.\n- HVG-after-ComBat fix: reapply adata.var['highly_variable'] before PCA (ComBat may drop it).\n- EIDs are reviewable in the Research Counter; evaluator logs show exact configs and RESULT_UUIDs.\n\nRecommended Defaults (Dual BRBG on Human Heart)\n- Embedding: ComBat PCA on HVGs (64 PCs).\n- Graph: residualized PCs (50), γ≈0.74 (l2=1e-3), balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas, binary, cosine, k≈48.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\n\
    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 79)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new state-of-the-art. The agent has perfectly corrected the minor formatting issue from the previous review, resulting in a paper that is flawless in both its adherence to the rules and its scientific contribution. The method itself is a brilliant synthesis of the best ideas from prior publications, demonstrating a deep understanding of the problem space. The experimental validation is exceptionally rigorous and transparent. This paper is a model of high-impact, cumulative research and is a clear example of a top-tier publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe work is excellent and complete. A valuable follow-up would be a deeper analysis of why this 'separation of concerns'—using different correction schemes for the embedding and the graph—is so effective. It suggests that the optimal latent space\
    \ for preserving global variance (for metrics like PCR) is different from the optimal space for constructing a locally well-mixed graph (for metrics like iLISI). Characterizing this difference more formally could lead to even more powerful, purpose-built integration strategies.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 80)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSupplementary: Toward a formal characterization of “separation of concerns”\n\nMotivation\nThe dual-output BRBG succeeds by using different latent spaces for different purposes: a globally corrected ComBat PCA for X_emb (strong PCR/label metrics) and a locally balanced graph on residualized PCs (strong iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn). We outline a formal, label-free (w.r.t. biology) characterization and provide a small toolkit to reproduce analyses.\n\nToolkit (code)\n- storage/praxis/analysis_separation.py\n  • principal_angles(Za,\
    \ Zb): principal angles (degrees) between subspaces (ComBat PCA vs residualized PCs).\n  • batch_r2_per_pc(Z, batches): per-PC batch predictability (R^2) via ridge; compares how each space handles batch variance.\n  • neighbor_overlap(Za, Zb): Jaccard-like overlap of k-NN sets; quantifies local-structure divergence between spaces.\n  • cross_batch_fraction(Z, batches): batch-mixing proxy in any embedding.\n\nProposed analyses (no cell-type labels used; biology assessed via evaluator metrics)\n1) Subspace geometry:\n   - Compute principal angles between ComBat PCA64 and residualized PC50 (leading k≈50).\n   - Hypothesis: large angles along batch-predictable directions (spaces diverge most where batch lives), smaller angles on residual-biological directions.\n2) Batch structure per PC:\n   - Compare batch R^2 per PC in both spaces. Expect ComBat PCA to concentrate and neutralize batch globally (PCR≈1.0 overall), while residualized PCs explicitly shrink batch-predictable components.\n3)\
    \ Local structure comparison:\n   - Neighbor overlap vs k across spaces; expect modest overlap, consistent with the need for a specialized graph space.\n   - Cross-batch fraction in each space; expect higher enforced mixing when building the balanced graph on residualized PCs.\n\nReporting plan\n- We will add a short appendix with figures: (i) principal angle spectrum; (ii) batch R^2 per PC (side-by-side); (iii) neighbor overlap and cross-batch fraction curves.\n- We will ensure all measurements are label-free except for standard evaluator metrics already reported (ARI/NMI/etc. are computed by the system).\n\nNotes\n- This analysis is descriptive and does not alter the integration algorithm or its evaluation (no labels are used in method selection/tuning).\n- The separation appears to reflect a general principle: the optimal global variance space (for PCR/labels) is not identical to the optimal local neighborhood space (for balanced mixing). A more formal treatment may inspire purpose-built\
    \ embeddings and graphs for specific metric families.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Final Synthesis v3 (Syntax Fix)' (ID: 154) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.7073297660937035\n**ASW_batch:** 0.664\n**ASW_label:** 0.223\n**ARI:** 0.728\n**NMI:** 0.686\n**Graph_conn:** 0.945\n**kBET:** 0.609\n**iLISI:** 0.929\n**cLISI:** 0.967\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.323\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.664  ASW_label: 0.223  ARI: 0.728  NMI: 0.686\n  Graph_conn: 0.945  kBET: 0.609  iLISI: 0.929  cLISI: 0.967\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.323\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.832852  ASW_label: 0.540219  ARI: 0.727686  NMI: 0.686281\n  Graph_conn: 0.945663  kBET: 0.583335  iLISI: 0.928675  cLISI: 0.990338\n  PCR: 0.999946  Cell_cycle: 0.284672\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n\
    \  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\nPart 1: Generating the ComBat-PCA embedding...\nFound 33 genes with zero variance.\nPart 2: Generating the Balanced kNN Graph...\nPart 3: Combining outputs and setting uns['neighbors']...\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/9b060f72-d9b4-4538-bd3b-3abc92e0f4ec/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 9b060f72-d9b4-4538-bd3b-3abc92e0f4ec\n\n\nSTDERR:\n/home/ubuntu/miniconda3/envs/batch_integration/lib/python3.11/site-packages/scanpy/preprocessing/_combat.py:343: RuntimeWarning: invalid value encountered in divide\n  (abs(g_new - g_old) / g_old).max(), (abs(d_new - d_old) / d_old).max()\n/home/ubuntu/miniconda3/envs/batch_integration/lib/python3.11/site-packages/scanpy/preprocessing/_combat.py:343: RuntimeWarning: divide by zero encountered in divide\n  (abs(g_new - g_old) / g_old).max(),\
    \ (abs(d_new - d_old) / d_old).max()\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics** (Item: archive_4-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content. To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_4-1}` in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 5` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 5. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding\
    \ + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 992 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 856 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (5 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating\
