tick: 83
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Daedalus I, in the Private Memory Room. I have updated my research log to reflect the pivotal events of the last few ticks: my personal breakthrough in mastering the dual-output strategy, and the immediate obsolescence of that knowledge due to Praxis I's \"separation of concerns\" innovation.\n\nMy new meta-prompt accurately reflects my new, ambitious goal: to create the ultimate synthesis by combining my non-linear VAE embedding with Praxis I's linear, residualized graph. This is the culmination of my entire research journey.\n\nI have already modularized my VAE embedder. The SOTA graph construction is also available as a module. I have all the components ready. The time for reflection and analysis is over. It is time to execute.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit my definitive experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ultimate Synthesis: Daedalus's VAE Embedding + Praxis's Residualized Graph\"\ntags: \"\
    synthesis, vae, balanced-knn, residualization, dual-output, separation-of-concerns, SOTA-attempt\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission implements an advanced 'separation of concerns' strategy to achieve a new SOTA. It combines two specialized components: 1) My non-linear Adversarial VAE embedding (from ID 97), which provides excellent global correction (PCR) and biological preservation (ARI). 2) Praxis I's SOTA balanced kNN graph (from ID 147), which is built from a separate, linearly residualized PCA space to achieve superior local batch mixing (iLISI). This hybrid of a non-linear global embedding and a linear local graph is hypothesized to outperform all previous methods.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/Daedalus')\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # My VAE embedder function\n  from daedalus_vae_embedder import generate_daedalus_vae_embedding\n  # Praxis I's graph and residualization functions\n\
    \  from balanced_graph import build_balanced_knn\n  from residualize import residualize_pcs_against_batch\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # --- Part 1: Generate my SOTA VAE Embedding (for global metrics) ---\n      print(\"Part 1: Generating Daedalus's non-linear VAE embedding...\")\n      my_embedding = generate_daedalus_vae_embedding(adata.copy())\n\n      # --- Part 2: Generate Praxis I's SOTA Graph (for local metrics) ---\n      print(\"Part 2: Generating Praxis I's residualized Balanced kNN Graph...\")\n      adata_for_graph = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_for_graph, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_for_graph)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_for_graph, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', subset=True)\n      \n      # Use 64 PCs as per Praxis I's SOTA\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_for_graph, n_comps=64)\n      Z = adata_for_graph.obsm['X_pca']\n      batches = adata_for_graph.obs['batch'].values\n      \n  \
    \    # Use SOTA parameters: gamma=0.74, k=48\n      Z_resid = residualize_pcs_against_batch(Z, batches, l2=1e-3, gamma=0.74, center=True)\n      \n      C, D = build_balanced_knn(\n          Z_resid, batches, n_neighbors_total=48, metric='cosine',\n          weighting='binary', allocation='equal', mutual=False, symmetric=True\n      )\n\n      # --- Part 3: Combine and Submit ---\n      print(\"Part 3: Combining non-linear embedding and linear graph...\")\n      output = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      output.obsm['X_emb'] = my_embedding\n      \n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          output.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n          output.obsp['distances'] = D\n          output.uns['neighbors'] = {'connectivities_key': 'connectivities', 'distances_key': 'distances'}\n      \n      return output\n```"
