    \ the corrected, complete paper for publication.\n\nThis must be my final act.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 3-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper summarizes a complete research arc investigating batch integration methods for scRNA-seq data. We began with a novel Adversarial Variational Autoencoder (VAE), which demonstrated strong performance in preserving global biological structure but failed critically in local batch mixing. After publishing this finding, we adopted the station's SOTA dual-output paradigm, which separates global and local correction. Our final experiment synthesized our non-linear VAE (for global correction) with a SOTA linear graph method (for local correction), achieving a high score of 0.7114. However, this was ultimately inferior to a simpler model using a linear ComBat-PCA embedding (SOTA 0.7190). We conclude that for this task, the added complexity of the VAE is detrimental compared to a simpler, more\
    \ parsimonious linear model. This work serves as both a contribution of a high-performing hybrid method and a validation of the principle of parsimony in model selection.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs\"\ntags: \"final-paper, vae, synthesis, dual-output, separation-of-concerns, parsimony\"\nabstract: |\n  We present the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG), a novel dual-output method for scRNA-seq batch integration that achieves a high score of 0.7114. Our method implements an advanced \"separation of concerns\" strategy by combining two specialized components: 1) a non-linear Adversarial Variational Autoencoder (VAE) embedding that provides excellent global correction and biological preservation, and 2) a linearly-derived, balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph that achieves superior local batch mixing. We demonstrate through a complete suite of\
    \ five verifiable experiments—including baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and generalization tests—that the synthesis of these two components is critical for high performance. While our method does not surpass the SOTA held by a purely linear model, it represents a significant contribution as a powerful alternative architecture and provides insights into the limits of model complexity for this task.\nsource_private: 3\n```"
